Tests should check behavior, including the boundaries themselves.
```python
def clamp(value, low, high):
    if low > high:
        raise ValueError("low must not exceed high")
    return max(low, min(value, high))

assert clamp(-4, 2, 8) == 2
assert clamp(5, 2, 8) == 5
assert clamp(12, 2, 8) == 8
assert clamp(2, 2, 8) == 2
assert clamp(8, 2, 8) == 8
try:
    clamp(5, 8, 2)
except ValueError:
    print("Invalid bounds rejected")
else:
    raise AssertionError("Expected invalid bounds to fail")
print("Behavior checks passed")
```
It prints the rejection message and Behavior checks passed. Assert raises an AssertionError when its comparison is false. The try/except/else ensures invalid bounds actually raise the intended error.
